  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 1,
    "lesson_title": "Time Compression",
    "lesson_core_move": "Map each time feature in $x(t)$ to t0/a in $x(at)$ for $a>1 and identify the resulting waveform as compressed toward t=0 with unchanged amplitudes$.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time Compression",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Time Compression",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "When the signal is written as $y(t)=x(at)$ with $a>1$, read the factor $a$ as a horizontal compression. Every time feature at $t_0$ in $x(t)$ appears at $t_0/a$ in $y(t)$, and the amplitudes stay the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(at)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the base case $y(t)=x(2t)$. Doubling the input time makes every feature happen at half its original time, so the waveform moves closer to $t=0$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(2t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The same rule works for any $a>1$: solve $at=t_0$ for the new time, which gives $t=t_0/a$. That is the location of every feature in the compressed waveform.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t=t_0/a",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the result is a compressed version of $x(t)$ squeezed toward $t=0$, with the horizontal spacing reduced and the amplitudes unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time Compression",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Recognizing $x(at)$ as Time Compression for $a>1$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Given $y(t)=x(4t)$, determine whether the signal is compressed or expanded and state what happens to the feature at $t_0=6$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Given $y(t)=x(4t)$, the factor $4>1$ means the signal is time-compressed toward $t=0$. The rule from the introduction applies: each time feature at $t_0$ moves to $t_0/4$ and keeps its amplitude.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(4t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the marked feature at $t_0=6$, solve the matching time equation $4t=6$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "4t=6",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That gives $t=6/4=1.5$, so the feature appears earlier in time but has the same height.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t=t_0/4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So $x(4t)$ is a compressed version of $x(t)$, with every time coordinate divided by $4$ and every amplitude unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time Compression",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Mapping a Single Time Feature Under Compression",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)$ has a peak at $t=6$, where does that peak appear in $x(3t)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the factor inside the argument: $x(3t)$ means $a=3>1$, so the feature moves toward $t=0$ by dividing its time coordinate by $3$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(3t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the peak at $t_0=6$ in $x(t)$, match the same feature by setting the input-time equation $3t=6$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "3t=6",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Solving gives $t=2$, so the peak now appears at $t=2$ in $x(3t)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "t=2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The peak height does not change; only its time location changes, so this is the same compression rule applied to one marked feature.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time Compression",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Compressing Support Intervals and Durations",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "If $x(t)$ is nonzero on $[2,10]$, what is the support of $y(t)=x(2t)$?",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the support interval of $x(t)$ and the factor $2$ in $y(t)=x(2t)$. Because $a=2>1$, each support endpoint moves toward $t=0$ by dividing its time coordinate by $2$, and the amplitudes stay the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(2t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the original support $[2,10]$. Map the left endpoint to $2/2=1$ and the right endpoint to $10/2=5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2/2=1,\\qquad 10/2=5",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the support of $y(t)$ is $[1,5]$. The duration changes from $10-2=8$ to $5-1=4$, which is half the original length, while the amplitudes stay unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "10-2=8,\\qquad 5-1=4",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the same compression rule applied to an entire interval: the support squeezes toward $t=0$, and only the horizontal spacing changes.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 1,
        "lesson_title": "Time Compression",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Reading a Compressed Waveform with Unchanged Amplitudes",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "A waveform $x(t)$ has a peak at $t=4$ and is nonzero on $[1,7]$. Describe the waveform $y(t)=x(2t)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the factor inside $y(t)=x(2t)$. Since $2>1$, read it as compression toward $t=0$: each time coordinate in $x(t)$ moves to half its original value, and the amplitudes stay the same.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "y(t)=x(2t)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Apply that rule to the peak at $t=4$: solve $2t=4$, so the peak moves to $t=2$. Apply the same rule to the support endpoints $1$ and $7$: they move to $1/2$ and $7/2$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "2t=4,\\qquad t=2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the full compressed waveform has the same peak height as $x(t)$, but its nonzero interval is now $[1/2,7/2]$. Everything has moved inward by the same horizontal factor.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "1\\mapsto 1/2,\\qquad 7\\mapsto 7/2",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In one sentence, $y(t)=x(2t)$ is the original waveform squeezed toward $t=0$ with unchanged amplitudes, a peak at $t=2$, and support $[1/2,7/2]$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
